{
  "result": "The laundromat on 5th still has those orange plastic chairs bolted to the floor. They're melted a little on one side from where someone left a dryer running too hot in '98, or so says Maria, who has owned the place for twenty years. I don't go there to do laundry anymore - I have a machine now - but I go there to think.\n\nThere's something honest about a laundromat. No one pretends to be doing anything else. You sit with your own clothes tumbling. You watch strangers fold their lives. A child's sock paired with a work uniform. A faded band t-shirt that has seen better decades. It's all circling, warm.\n\nOutside, the world insists on speed, on optimization, on becoming. Inside, you just wait. The dryers hum a low, patient chord. Maria's radio plays oldies. The soap vending machine clunks.\n\nI think we don't have enough places that force us to wait without also selling us something to scroll. Laundromats are one of the last secular monasteries. You bring what's dirty. You are not judged for it. You sit. You watch it get clean.\n\nAnd then you leave, carrying something warm against your chest on a cold night, feeling, for a minute, like you have your life mostly together. It's not much, but on nights when everything feels frayed, that small, borrowed heat is enough to get you home. Maria always waves goodbye like she knows you'll be back. And eventually, you always are.",
